==English==
{{was wotd|2024|July|12}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpʌb.lɪk/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-public.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpʊb.lɪk/|a=Ireland,Northern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɐb.lɪk/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɐb.lək/|a=NZ}}
* {{hyphenation|en|publ|ic}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌblɪk|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pleh₁-}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} and {{glossary|noun}} are derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|publik}}, {{m|enm|publike|t=(''adjective'') generally observable, public; relating to the general public or public affairs; (''noun'') a generally observable place or situation}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=pū̆blik(e|pos=adj|id=MED35134}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|public}}, {{m|xno|publik}}, {{m|xno|publique}}, {{der|en|frm|public}}, {{m|frm|publique}}, and {{der|en|fro|public|t=(''adjective'') generally observable, public; relating to the general public; official; (''noun'') community or its members collectively; nation, state; audience, spectators collectively}} (modern {{cog|fr|public}}, {{m|fr|publique}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}); and from their {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|pūblicus|t=of or belonging to the community, people, or state; general, public}}, an alteration of {{m|la|poplicus}} (influenced by {{m|la|pūbēs|t=adult men; male population}}), from {{m|la|poplus|t=community; the people, public; nation, state}} (later {{m|la|populus}}; from {{der|en|itc-pro|*poplos|t=army}}; further origin uncertain, possibly from {{der|en|ett|-}} or from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pleh₁-|t=to fill}}) + {{m|la|-icus|pos={{glossary|suffix}} meaning ‘of or pertaining to’}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=5287184156|date=June 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj.'' and ''n|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220704124559/https://www.lexico.com/definition/public}}</ref> Related to {{m|en|people}}, {{m|en|populus}}{{,}} etc.

The Middle English word displaced native {{noncog|ang|ceorlfolc}} and {{m|ang|folclic}}.

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the adjective.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=4991342587|date=July 2023}}</ref>

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{senseid|en|open to general view}} [[able#Adjective|Able]] to be [[know#Verb|known]] or [[see#Verb|seen]] by [[everyone]]; [[happen]]ing without [[concealment]]; [[open#Adjective|open]] to [[general#Adjective|general]] [[view#Noun|view]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|act=I|scene=vi|page=100|column=1|passage=VVith ſcoffes and ſcornes, and contumelious taunts, / In open Market-place produc't they me, / To be a '''publique''' ſpectacle to all: / Here, ſayd they, is the Terror of the French, / The Scar-Crovv that affrights our Children ſo.}}
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws|year=1611|chapter=S. [[w:Paul the Apostle|Pauls]] Writing is No More Preaching, then His Pen or his Hand is His Toong: Seeing They Cannot be the Same which Cannot be Made by the Same Instruments|page=222|passage=[T]he Apoſtles preached as vvell vvhen they vvrote as vvhen they ſpake the Goſpell of [[w:Jesus|Chriſt]], and our vſuall '''''publique''' reading'' of the vvord of God for the peoples inſtruction ''is preaching''.}}
#* {{RQ:Hall Contemplations|volume=V|chapter=The Marriage in Cana|page=452|passage=Thy {{quote-gloss|{{w|Jesus}}'s}} firſt '''publique''' miracle graceth a marriage; It is an ancient and laudable inſtitution, that the rites of matrimony ſhould not vvant a ſolemne celebration; VVhen are feaſts in ſeaſon, if not at the recouery of our loſt ribbe?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|translator=[[w:William Lower (dramatist)|William Lower]]|title=A Relation in Form of Journal, of the Voiage and Residence which the Most Excellent and Most Mighty Prince Charls the II King of Great Britain, &c. hath Made in Holland, from the 25 of May, to the 2 of June, 1660.{{nb...|Rendered into English out of the Original French, by Sir William Lower, Knight.}}|location=The Hague|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:Adriaan Vlacq|Adrian Vlack]]|year=1660|page=4|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/relationinformof00lowe/page/n17/mode/1up|oclc=78891221|passage=The Parliament alſo permitted General [[w:George Monck, 1st Duke of Albemarle|{{quote-gloss|George}} Monck]] to ſend M{{sup|r}} [[w:Thomas Clarges|{{quote-gloss|Thomas}} Clarges]] his brother-in-law, accompanied vvith ſome Officers of the Army, to aſſure his Majeſty {{quote-gloss|{{w|Charles II of England}}}} of the fidelity and obedience of the Army; vvhich had made '''publick''' and ſolemn proteſtations thereof, after the Letter and Declaration vvas communicated unto them by the General.}}
#* {{RQ:Tatler|pseudonym=Bickerstaff|issue=11|date=5 May 1709|page=73|passage=[O]ur last advices from Spain inform us, that the prince of Asturias had made his '''public''' entry into Madrid in great splendour.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=58–59|pageref=58|passage=Clear away! There was nothing they wouldn't have cleared away, or couldn't have cleared away, with old Fezziwig looking on. It was done in a minute. Every movable was packed off, as if it were dismissed from '''public''' life for evermore; the floor was swept and watered, the lamps were trimmed, fuel was heaped upon the fire; and the warehouse was as snug, and warm, and dry, and bright a ball-room, as you would desire to see upon a winter's night.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Sandra Laville|title=[[w:Paul Stephenson (police officer)|Paul Stephenson]] returns to desk at Metropolitan police|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20231025092158/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2011/apr/18/paul-stephenson-returns-metropolitan-police|date=18 April 2011|passage=Earlier this month [[w:Tim Godwin|{{quote-gloss|Tim}} Godwin]] had to make a '''public''' apology to the family of Daniel Morgan after the collapse of a £30m inquiry into his murder in 1987.}}
# {{senseid|en|communal}} Open to all [[member#Noun|members]] of a [[community]], [[as opposed to]] only a [[segment#Noun|segment]] of it; especially, [[provide]]d by [[national#Adjective|national]] or [[local#Adjective|local]] [[authority|authorities]] and [[support#Verb|supported]] by [[money#Noun|money]] from [[tax#Noun|taxes]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{coi|en|'''public''' library&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; '''public''' park}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|act=II|scene=ii|page=347|column=2|passage=I ſavv her once / Hop forty Paces through the '''publicke''' ſtreete, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Gaskell Mary Barton|volume=I|chapter=I|page=1|passage={{smallcaps|There}} are some fields near Manchester, well known to the inhabitants as "Green Heys Fields," through which runs a '''public''' footpath to a little village about two miles distant.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=A Visit to the Asylum for Aged and Decayed Punsters|journal=[[w:The Atlantic|The Atlantic Monthly. A Magazine of Literature, Art, and Politics]]|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher={{w|Ticknor and Fields}},{{nb...|135 Washington Street}}; London: [[w:Nicholas Trübner|Trübner and Company]]|month=January|year=1861|volume=VII|issue=XXXIX|page=114|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/atlantic07bostuoft/atlantic07bostuoft/page/114/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=932565813|passage=Our late distinguished townsman, Noah Dow, Esquire, as is well known, bequeathed a large portion of his fortune to this establishment,—"being thereto moved," as his will expressed it, "by the desire of ''N. Dowing'' some '''publick''' Institution for the benefit of Mankind."}}
#* {{RQ:Buchan Three Hostages|chapter=The House in Gospel Oak|page=85|passage=Then it occurred to me that I might be doing a rash thing in going off to an unknown house in a seedy suburb. So I went into a '''public''' telephone-booth, rang up the Club, and told the porter that if Colonel Arbuthnot called, I was at 4 Palmyra Square, N.W.—I made him write down the address—and would be back before ten o'clock.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=[[w:David Smith (journalist)|David Smith]]|title=South African ‘baby safe’ condemned by child welfare groups|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516205722/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/may/10/south-africa-baby-safe-condemned|date=10 May 2011|passage=Officials say about 500 babies are abandoned each year in Western Cape province and that the number is increasing. Some are left for dead on rubbish tips, in refuge bags or at '''public''' toilets.}}
## {{senseid|en|company}}{{lb|en|business}} Of a [[company#Noun|company]]: having [[share#Noun|shares]] of [[stock#Noun|stock]] [[trade#Verb|traded]] [[publicly]], for example, through a [[stock market]].
# {{senseid|en|popular}} [[pertain|Pertaining]] to the [[person#Noun|people]] [[as a whole]], as opposed to a [[group#Noun|group]] of people; concerning the whole community or [[country#Noun|country]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Glanvill Witches|page=5|passage=[S]tanding '''publick''' ''Records'' have been kept of theſe vvell atteſted Relations, and ''Epocha''’s made of thoſe unvvonted events.}}
#* {{RQ:Ray Low-countries|chapter=Of Venice|page=163|passage=[I]f the nominated be to a place vvherein he is entruſted vvith the management of '''public''' moneys, he that nominates is ſurety for him, and is to make good vvhat he defrauds the Common-vvealth of.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Modest Proposal|page=16|passage=I Profeſs in the ſincerity of my Heart that I have not the leaſt perſonal Intereſt in endeavouring to promote this neceſſary VVorks having no other Motive than the '''''publick''' Good of my Country'', by ''advancing our Trade, providing for Infants, relieving the Poor, and giving ſome Pleaſure to the Rich.''}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Works|volume=VIII|title=Epistle to Gay|page=118|passage=I knew a brazen minister of state, / Who bore for twice ten years the '''publick''' hate. / In every mouth the question most in vogue / Was, When will they turn out this odious rogue?}}
#* {{RQ:Mill Political Economy|volume=I|chapter=Preliminary Remarks|page=8|passage=Money, being the instrument of an important '''public''' and private purpose, is rightly regarded as wealth; but everything else which serves any human purpose, and which nature does not afford gratuitously, is wealth also. {{...}} Everything forms therefore a part of wealth, which has a power of purchasing; for which anything useful or agreeable would be given in exchange.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Adam Vaughan|title=Public awareness of the biodiversity crisis is virtually non-existent|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20231209234150/https://www.theguardian.com/environment/blog/2010/sep/16/public-awareness-biodiversity-crisis|date=16 September 2010|passage=A mere 3% of the more than 1,000 people interviewed said they actually knew what the conference was about. It seems safe to say '''public''' awareness of the Convention on Biological Awareness in Nagoya&nbsp;– and its goal of safeguarding wildlife&nbsp;– is close to non-existent.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:George Monbiot|title=Why the politics of envy are keenest among the very rich [print version: Money just makes the rich suffer, 17 May 2013]|journal=w:The Guardian Weekly|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221016141734/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/may/06/politics-envy-keenest-rich|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=6 May 2013|volume=188|issue=23|page=19|issn=0959-3608|oclc=1060180436|passage=In order to grant the rich these pleasures, the social contract is reconfigured. {{...}} The '''public''' realm is privatised, the regulations restraining the ultra-wealthy and the companies they control are abandoned, and Edwardian levels of inequality are almost fetishised.}}
# {{senseid|en|representative}} [[officially|Officially]] [[represent]]ing the community; [[carry out|carried out]] or [[fund#Verb|funded]] by the [[government]] or [[state#Noun|state]] on behalf of the community, rather than by a [[private#Adjective|private]] [[organization]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{coi|en|'''public''' housing&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; '''public''' officer&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; '''public''' prosecutor&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; '''public''' servant}}
#* {{RQ:Suetonius Holland Caesars|section=20|page=8|passage=Hee brought-in likevvise the ancient cuſtome againe, that in vvhat moneth hee had not the Knitches of rods vvith Axes borne before him, a '''publique''' Officer called {{smallcaps|Accensvs}} ſhould huiſher him before, and the Serjeants or Lictours follovv after behinde.}}
#* {{RQ:Ray Low-countries|chapter=Of Venice|page=170|passage=[T]hoſe vvho aſſiſt the Commonvvealth in a time of need vvith their eſtates, lending ſuch a ſum of mony as the Lavv determines, have liberty granted them to be preſent in this Council, and to underſtand the management of '''public''' affairs (yet vvithout povver of balloting) till ſuch time as their moneys be repaid, and ſometimes longer.}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Pamela|volume=IV|letter=X|chapter=From Miss Darnford to Her Father and Mother|pages=59–60|pageref=60|passage=[P]oor Houſekeepers, vvho vvill be glad to accept of ſome private Benefactions, and yet, having lived creditably, till reduced by Misfortunes, are aſhamed to apply for '''publick''' Relief: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|quotee=Johnson|year=1783|page=440|passage=It is vvonderful, Sir, vvith hovv little real ſuperiority of mind men can make an eminent figure in '''publick''' life.}}
#* {{RQ:Alcott Little Women|part=2|page=162|passage=Eager to find material for stories, and bent on making them original in plot, if not masterly in execution, {{...}} she excited the suspicions of '''public''' librarians by asking for works on poisons; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XLIV|page=356|passage=From another point of view, it was a place without a soul. The well-to-do had hearts of stone; the rich were brutally bumptious; the Press, the Municipality, all the '''public''' men, were ridiculously, vaingloriously self-satisfied.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|title=Economic policy: For art's sake|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171122191639/https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2004/jun/18/economy.artsfunding||date=18 Jun 2004|passage=But culture's total budget is a tiny proportion of all '''public''' spending; it is one of the government's most visible success stories.}}
# Pertaining to a person in the [[capacity]] in which they [[deal with]] other people on a  [[formal#Adjective|formal]] or [[official#Adjective|official]] [[basis]], as opposed to a [[personal#Adjective|personal]] or private capacity; official, [[professional#Adjective|professional]].
#: {{coi|en|'''public''' face&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; '''public''' image}}
#* {{RQ:Tatler|author=Steele|pseudonym=Distaff|issue=10|date=3 May 1709|page=60|passage=The first that I lay my hands on, is a treatise concerning 'the empire of beauty,' and the effects it has had in all nations of the world, upon the '''public''' and private actions of men; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Mansfield Park|volume=III|page=67|passage=The preacher {{...}} who can say any thing new or striking, any thing that rouses the attention, without offending the taste, or wearing out the feelings of his hearers, is a man whom one could not (in his '''public''' capacity) honour enough.}}
# {{senseid|en|programming}}{{lb|en|not comparable|by extension|object-oriented programming}} Of an [[object#Noun|object]]: [[accessible]] to the [[program#Noun|program]] [[in general]], not only to a [[class#Noun|class]] or [[subclass]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}}
## Pertaining to [[nation#Noun|nations]] [[collectively]], or to nations [[regard#Verb|regarded]] as [[civilized#Adjective|civilized]]; [[international#Adjective|international]], [[supernational]].
##* {{RQ:Erasmus Newe Testamente|tome=1|book=John|chapter=VII|folio=lviii|passage={{...}} {{w|Nicodemus}} had not affirmed him {{quote-gloss|{{w|Jesus}}}} to be a prophete, but ſayde: whoſoeuer he be, he ought not after our common, or rather '''publike''' law, (that is to ſaye, a lawe which indifferently perteyneth to all men of euery ſtate) to be condemned, except his cauſe be knowen before.}}
##* {{RQ:Boyle Occasional Reflections|part=2|chapter=XVII|chaptername=Upon Ones Talking to an Eccho|page=117|passage=[S]uch a one concerns himſelf very needleſsly for almoſt all the '''publique''' Quarrels in Chriſtendome, and ſhevvs himſelf zealous for a party vvhich vvill receive no advantage by his diſquiets; {{...}}}}
## ''Now chiefly in'' '''[[public spirit]]''' ''and'' '''[[public-spirited]]''': [[seek#Verb|seeking]] to [[further#Verb|further]] the [[good#Adjective|best]] [[interest#Noun|interests]] or [[well-being]] of the community or nation.
##* {{RQ:Giraffi Howell Naples|part=II|chapter=The Second Tumults Happened in the City of Naples, Held to be Hotter and Higher than the Former, which Succeeded the 7. of July|page=43|passage=[T]he ſaid {{quote-gloss|Joseph}} ''Palumbo'' vvas reſtrain'd to Saint ''Lorenzo''; but being a popular man, and one knovvn to be a good Patriot, and of a '''publick''' ſoul, and a perſon of integrity; there vvere four thouſand of the beſt armed men joyn'd together, to vindicate and free the ſaid ''Palumbo'', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Howard Four New Plays|page=161|passage=Suppoſe I ſhou'd ſtrike firſt, vvou'd it not breed / Grief in your '''publick''' heart to ſee her bleed?}}
##* {{RQ:Thomson Winter|year=1768|lines=593–597|pages=185–186|pageref=186|passage=As thus vve talk'd, / Our hearts vvould burn vvithin us, vvould inhale / That portion of divinity, that ray / Of pureſt heaven, vvhich lights the '''public''' ſoul / Of patriots, and of heroes.}}
##* {{RQ:Emerson Representative Men|pages=228–229|pageref=229|passage=Napoleon had been the first man of the world, if his ends had been purely '''public'''.}}
## ''Now only in'' '''[[public figure]]''': [[famous#Adjective|famous]], [[prominent#Adjective|prominent]], [[well-known]].
##* {{RQ:Defoe Jack|page=340|passage=I {{smallcaps|VVas}} not ſo '''publick''' here, as to be very vvell knovvn, at leaſt by any one that had Knovvledge of me in the Country vvhere I liv'd; and this vvas indeed my ſafety aftervvard, as you vvill ſoon hear; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|UK|education|chiefly|historical}} In some [[old#Adjective|older]] [[university|universities]] in the [[United Kingdom]]: open or pertaining to the whole university, as opposed to a [[constituent#Adjective|constituent]] [[college]] or an [[individual#Adjective|individual]] [[staff#Adjective|staff]] member or [[student]].
#* {{RQ:Erasmus Udall Apophthegmes|chapter=Socrates|folio=4|para=11|passage=I meruailled, why himſelf {{quote-gloss|{{w|Niccolò Leoniceno}}}} did not practiſe Phiſike of whiche facultee he was a Doctour, and a '''publique''' reader: I auaile moche more, ſaieth he, in that I teach all thother Phiſiciãs {{quote-gloss|physicians}}.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}}
## Of or pertaining to the [[human race]] as a whole; [[common#Adjective|common]], [[universal]].
##* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=1|lines=639–640|page=68|passage=In Iron Clouds conceal'd the '''Publick''' Light: / And Impious Mortals fear'd Eternal Night.}}
##* {{RQ:Hawthorne French and Italian Notebooks|volume=I|chapter=The Mediterranean Sea|date=17 January 1858|page=54|passage=In the squares and places you see half-a-dozen of them together, sitting in a social circle on the bottoms of upturned baskets, knitting, talking, and enjoying the '''public''' sunshine, as if it were their own household fire.}}
## ''Chiefly in'' '''[[make#Verb|make]] public''': of a [[work#Noun|work]]: [[printed#Adjective|printed]] or otherwise [[published#Adjective|published]].
##* {{RQ:Robertson America|volume=II|note=I|page=441|passage={{smallcaps|The}} firſt of his {{quote-gloss|{{w|Hernán Cortés}}'s}} diſpatches has never been made '''public'''. It vvas ſent from Vera-Cruz, July 16th, 1519.}}

