====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|THE}} {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} ''Chiefly preceded by'' '''[[the#Article|the]]''': [[member#Noun|members]] of the [[community]] or the [[person#Noun|people]] [[in general]], regardless of [[membership]] of any [[particular#Adjective|particular]] [[group#Noun|group]].
#: {{ux|en|Members of the '''public''' may not proceed beyond this point.}}
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=19|folio=325|passage=In ſũ {{quote-gloss|sum}}, you are a Prince, & a father of people, vvho ought vvith the eye of vviſdome, the hand of fortitude, and the hart of iuſtice to ſet dovvne all priuate conceits, in compariſon of vvhat for the '''publike''' is profitable.}}
#* {{RQ:Jonson Catiline His Conspiracy|sig=O|verso=1|page=113|passage=Here, you, the ſad reuengers / Of capitall crimes, againſt the '''Publicke''', take / This man vnto your iuſtice: ſtrangle him.}}
#* {{RQ:Boyle Occasional Reflections|part=2|chapter=XI|chaptername=Upon a Danger Springing from an Unseasonable Contest with the Steersman|page=78|passage=[I]t is not only requiſite that the Prince knovv hovv to command vvell, but that the Subjects obey vvell; and that even vveak Counſels, faithfully aſſiſted, and as much as may be rectified or repaired by thoſe that are to Execute them, may leſs prejudice the '''publick''', than the frovvard and jarring endeavours of Men, that perhaps vvould be vviſer Rulers if they had a right to be so.}}
#* {{RQ:Ray Low-countries|chapter=Of Venice|page=154|passage=And though the '''public''' be not ſo rich as it hath been, yet vvill it ſoon recover itſelf and grovv vvealthy again upon the enjoyment of Peace and free Commerce.}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|book=IV|chapter=Of Offences against the Public Peace|page=151|passage=[T]he only facts to be conſidered {{quote-gloss|in the crime of libel}} are, firſt, the making or publiſhing of the book or vvriting; and ſecondly, vvhether the matter be criminal: and, if both theſe points are againſt the defendant, the offence againſt the '''public''' is complete.}}
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Tremarn Case|section=2|passage=Two or three months more went by; the '''public''' were eagerly awaiting the arrival of this semi-exotic claimant to an English peerage, and sensations, surpassing those of the {{w|Tichborne case}}, were looked forward to with palpitating interest.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=w:Martin Jacques|title=[[w:Nicolas Sarkozy|Sarkozy]] plays the race card&nbsp;– and our establishment cheers|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220808054906/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2007/may/04/comment.france|date=May 4 2007|passage=[[w:George W. Bush|{{quote-gloss|George Walker}} Bush]] and [[w:Tony Blair|{{quote-gloss|Tony}} Blair]] stand condemned by their own '''publics''' and face imminent political extinction.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}}
## ''Preceded by a {{glossary|possessive determiner}} such as'' '''[[my#Determiner|my]]''''','' '''[[your#Determiner|your]]''''', or'' '''[[their#Determiner|their]]''': a group of people who [[support#Verb|support]] a particular person, especially a [[performer]], a [[writer]], etc.; an [[audience]], a [[following#Noun|following]].
##: {{hyponyms|en|readership|viewership}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Quentin Durward|volume=I|page=xiii|passage=By dint of drinking acid tiff, as above mentioned, and smoking segars, in which I am no novice, my '''Public''' are to be informed, that I gradually drank and smoked myself into a certain degree of acquaintance with ''un homme comme il faut'' {{quote-gloss|a decent man}}, one of the few fine old specimens of nobility who are still to be found in France; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} {{ellipsis of|en|public house,public bar<l:dated>|t=an inn, a pub: the more basic bar in a public house, as contrasted with the lounge bar or [[saloon]] bar which has more comfortable seats, personalized service, etc.}}.
##: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:pub}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Redgauntlet|volume=II|chapter=Narrative of Alan Fairford, Continued|page=291|passage=[T]hese inconsiderate lads will be out of the house, and away to the '''publicks''', wasting their precious time, and, it may be, missing the morning tide.}}
##* {{RQ:Hughes Tom Brown|passage=[I]t is a terrible long and slippery descent, and a shocking bad road. At the bottom, however, there is a pleasant '''public'''; whereat we must really take a modest quencher, for the down air is provocative of thirst.}}
## {{lb|en|often|[[public relations]]}} ''Often preceded by'' '''the''' ''and a [[qualify]]ing [[word#Noun|word]]'': a particular [[demographic#Noun|demographic]] or group of people, or [[segment#Noun|segment]] of the [[population]], [[share#Verb|sharing]] some [[common#Adjective|common]] [[characteristic#Noun|characteristic]].
##: {{coi|en|the cinema-going '''public'''}}
##: {{coi|en|the reading '''public'''}}
##* {{RQ:Coleridge Biographia Literaria|volume=II|page=4|passage=But year after year increased the number of Mr. [[w:William Wordsworth|{{quote-gloss|William}} Wordsworth]]'s admirers. They were found too not in the lower classes of the reading '''public''', but chiefly among young men of strong sensibility and meditative minds; and their admiration (inflamed perhaps in some degree by opposition) was distinguished by its intensity, I might almost say, by its ''religious'' fervour.}}
##* {{RQ:Ruskin Arrows|volume=I|chapter=Art Criticism|date='''1843''' December? (date written; published '''1843''' January)|noformat=1|page=21|passage=People continually forget that there is a ''separate'' '''public''' for every picture, and for every book. Appealed to with reference to any particular work, the '''public''' is that class of persons who possess the knowledge which it presupposes, and the faculties to which it is addressed. With reference to a new edition of [[w:Isaac Newton|{{quote-gloss|Isaac}} Newton]]'s [[w:Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica|Principia]], the "'''public'''" means little more than the Royal Society. With reference to one of [[w:William Wordsworth|{{quote-gloss|William}} Wordsworth]]'s poems, it means all who have hearts.}}
### {{lb|en|sociology}} A group of people sharing some common [[cultural]], [[political#Adjective|political]], or [[social#Adjective|social]] [[interest#Noun|interest]], but not [[necessarily]] having any [[interaction]]s with each other.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### ''Chiefly preceded by'' '''the''': a [[collective#Adjective|collective]] [[body#Noun|body]] of a [[politically]] [[organized#Adjective|organized]] [[nation#Noun|nation]] or [[state#Noun|state]]; a [[body politic]], a nation, a state; also, the interest or [[well-being]] of such a collective body; the [[common good]].
###: {{sense|well-being}} {{synonyms|en|commonweal<q:archaic or obsolete>|public interest|public good}}
### {{lb|en|US|university slang}} At {{w|Harvard University}}: a [[penalty]] [[impose#Verb|imposed]] on a [[student]] [[involve|involving]] a [[grade#Noun|grade]] [[reduction]] which is [[communicate]]d to the student's [[parent#Noun|parents]] or [[guardian]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} ''Chiefly in'' '''[[in public]]''': the [[presence#Noun|presence]] of [[spectator]]s or people [[generally]]; the [[open#Noun|open]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=II|scene=i|page=283|column=1|passage=Come follovv vs, / VVe are to ſpeake in '''publique''': for this buſineſſe / VVill raiſe vs all.}}
#* {{RQ:Evelyn Diary|volume=I|month=June|year=1645|pages=191–192|pageref=191|passage=[W]e went to the Chetto de san Felice, to see the noblemen and their ladies at ''Basset'', a game at cards which is much used, but they play not in '''public''', and all that have inclination to it are in masquerade, without speaking one word, and so they come in, play, loose or gaine, and go away as they please.}}
#* {{RQ:Burney Evelina|volume=I|letter=XXII|lettername=Evelina [to the Rev. Mr. Villars] in Continuation|pages=177–178|pageref=178|passage=[S]he ſcolded me for I believe tvvo hours, on account of having left her, {{...}} But ſhe aſſured me, that if ever I did ſo again, ſhe vvould never more take me into '''public'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma|volume=II|page=267|passage=[M]y particular friend, Mrs. Partridge, the lady I have always resided with when in Bath, would be most happy to shew you any attentions, and would be the very person for you to go into '''public''' with.}}
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton What will He Do with It|volume=I|chapter=I|page=6|passage=[K]isses, though pleasant in private, are insipid in '''public'''.}}

